      "internal": false,
      "mergeID": "62f6131dc48f1944b3cb0d3a",
      "mergedPostDetails": "Mirrors currently have a maximum resolution of 2048 by 2048. Because of this, mirror reflections have noticeably degraded quality when running at high resolutions, which is often the case if you are using VR headsets such as the HP Reverb G2, Vive Pro 2, Valve Index, Quest 2. I propose three possible ways address this issue:\n 1: Remove the mirror cap. This is the simplest solution, but exposes the player to any performance issues that you might have been guarding against.\n 2: Add an option in VRChat's configuration file to adjust the mirror cap. This allows you to leave the mirror cap as is, but lets advanced users adjust it if they are so inclined.\n 3: Remove the mirror cap, but add an option in the UI to set the mirror resolution to a percentage of the current display resolution. This solution has the advantage of being more user-friendly than second option.\nAdditionally, if you do add an option to raise or lower the mirror cap, you should consider also adding an option to set the MSAA level for mirrors as well. Aliasing artifacts are more apparent in a lower resolution mirror, so it follows that one might want to have a higher MSAA level for mirrors than for regular rendering.",
      "mergedPostTitle": "Configuration option for mirror resolution cap",
